Here's an HTML template with Jinja2 tags and `<script>` tags:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>{{ title }}</title>
    <style>
        body {
            font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
            line-height: 1.6;
            margin: 0;
            padding: 20px;
            background-color: #f4f4f4;
        }
        .container {
            max-width: 800px;
            margin: 0 auto;
            overflow: hidden;
            background: #fff;
            padding: 20px;
            border-radius: 5px;
            box-shadow: 0 0 10px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1);
        }
        h1 {
            color: #333;
        }
        .content {
            margin-bottom: 20px;
        }
    </style>
</head>
<body>
    <div class="container">
        <h1>{{ heading }}</h1>
        <div class="content">
            {% if content %}
                {{ content|safe }}
            {% else %}
                <p>No content available.</p>
            {% endif %}
        </div>

        {% if show_button %}
            <button id="actionButton">Click Me</button>
        {% endif %}

        <script>
            document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', function() {
                const button = document.getElementById('actionButton');
                if (button) {
                    button.addEventListener('click', function() {
                        alert('Button clicked!');
                        // You can also call a Jinja2 variable here if it's passed as data
                        {% if button_message %}
                            console.log("{{ button_message }}");
                        {% endif %}
                    });
                }
            });
        </script>
    </div>
</body>
</html>
```

### Key Features:
1. **Jinja2 Variables**:
   - `{{ title }}`, `{{ heading }}`, `{{ content }}` (with `|safe` filter)
   - `{{ button_message }}` inside the script

2. **Jinja2 Conditions**:
   - `{% if content %}` / `{% else %}` for conditional content rendering
   - `{% if show_button %}` for conditional button rendering

3. **Script Integration**:
   - JavaScript event listener for the button
   - Access to Jinja2 variables inside the script (e.g., `{{ button_message }}`)

4. **Basic Styling**:
   - Simple CSS for layout and appearance

You can customize this template further based on your specific needs!

Here's a C function that uses bitwise operators, followed by an example of the pipe operator in a shell command:

```c
#include <stdio.h>

// Function that demonstrates bitwise operations
unsigned int bitwise_operations(unsigned int a, unsigned int b) {
    // Left shift: multiply a by 2^3 (8)
    unsigned int shifted_a = a << 3;

    // Right shift: divide b by 2^2 (4), preserving sign for unsigned
    unsigned int shifted_b = b >> 2;

    // Bitwise OR: combine bits of shifted_a and shifted_b
    unsigned int or_result = shifted_a | shifted_b;

    // Bitwise AND: find common set bits between shifted_a and shifted_b
    unsigned int and_result = shifted_a & shifted_b;

    return or_result + and_result;  // Return sum of both operations
}

int main() {
    unsigned int num1 = 5;   // Binary: 0101
    unsigned int num2 = 12;  // Binary: 1100

    printf("Result: %u\n", bitwise_operations(num1, num2));
    return 0;
}
```

Example of the pipe operator in a shell command:
```bash
ls -l | grep ".txt"
```
This command lists files in long format (`ls -l`) and pipes the output to `grep` which filters lines containing ".txt".

